Using a fixed sample of 500 puzzles from \texttt{sapientinc/sudoku-extreme}, we systematically benchmark:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Direct LLM Reasoning:} Frontier LLMs (Claude Opus 5.5, Sonnet 5.5, and Haiku 5.5) prompted to solve extreme puzzles directly using full test-time adaptive thinking (up to 32,000 tokens).

Our experiments, conducted entirely on a single consumer laptop CPU with an LLM API budget of under $\$4.00$, yield several definitive insights:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Direct autoregressive reasoning is unreliable and uneconomical:} Direct reasoning achieves only $30\%\text{--}60\%$ solve rates.

A valid board satisfies:
\begin{equation}
x_i \neq x_j, \quad \forall i \in \{0, \dots, 80\}, \ \forall j \in \mathcal{N}(i).

The energy of a belief state $\vP$ is defined by:
\begin{equation}
E(\vP) = \sum_{i=1}^{81} \sum_{d=1}^9 P_{i, d} \log P_{i, d} + \frac{1}{2} \sum_{i=1}^{81} \sum_{j \neq i} \sum_{d=1}^9 \sum_{d'=1}^9 W_{i, j, d, d'} P_{i, d} P_{j, d'} + \sum_{i=1}^{81} \sum_{d=1}^9 b_{i, d} P_{i, d},

For each cell $i$, the model minimizes the cross-entropy between the true digit $x_i$ and the conditionally normalized logit distribution:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}(\mW, \vb) = - \frac{1}{K} \sum_{k=1}^K \sum_{i=1}^{81} \log \frac{\exp \left( - b_{i, x_i^{(k)}} - \sum_{j \neq i} W_{i, j, x_i^{(k)}, x_j^{(k)}} \right)}{\sum_{d=1}^9 \exp \left( - b_{i, d} - \sum_{j \neq i} W_{i, j, d, x_j^{(k)}} \right)}.

Here, the pairwise interaction tensor requires no training data and is parameterized by a single positive scalar penalty $w_0 > 0$:
\begin{equation}
W^{\text{hand}}_{i, j, d, d'} = \begin{cases} 
w_0 & \text{if } j \in \mathcal{N}(i) \text{ and } d = d',

0 & \text{otherwise},
\end{cases}
\qquad b^{\text{hand}}_{i, d} = 0.

We evaluate two starkly contrasting modalities for frontier LLMs:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Direct Chain-of-Thought (CoT) Reasoning:} The puzzle is provided as an 81-character string or $9 \times 9$ ASCII grid.

The empirical results reveal severe limitations of purely autoregressive reasoning:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Token Exhaustion on Identical Hard Puzzles:} Both Opus 5.5 and Sonnet 5.5 failed on the exact same two puzzles (difficulty ratings 88 and 15).

